            <paragraph><content styleCode="Bold">CT ABDOMEN AND PELVIS WITHOUT CONTRAST</content> (performed October 18, 2025 07:52 UTC; accession SRM-CT-2510-88412)</paragraph>
            <paragraph>Indication: Right flank pain radiating to the groin, hematuria.</paragraph>
            <paragraph>Technique: Helical CT of the abdomen and pelvis without intravenous or oral contrast. Dose reduction technique was used.</paragraph>
            <paragraph>Findings: There is a 4 mm calculus at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydroureteronephrosis and mild perinephric stranding. No other renal or ureteral calculi. The left kidney is unremarkable. The liver, gallbladder, spleen, pancreas and adrenal glands are unremarkable for a non-contrast study. The appendix is normal. No bowel obstruction. No free fluid. The urinary bladder is decompressed. Uterus and adnexa are unremarkable. Lung bases: a 7 mm solid nodule is present in the right lower lobe (series 2, image 14). No pleural effusion. No acute osseous abnormality.</paragraph>
            <paragraph>Impression: 1. 4 mm obstructing calculus at the right ureterovesical junction with mild right hydroureteronephrosis. 2. Incidental 7 mm solid pulmonary nodule in the right lower lobe, partially imaged. Per Fleischner Society guidelines, a non-contrast CT of the chest is recommended in 6-12 months.</paragraph>
            <paragraph>Electronically signed by Hannah Lindqvist, MD (Radiology), October 18, 2025 08:31 UTC.</paragraph>
          </text>
